EXECUTION DECISION — this story invokes the EARLY-LAND exception, and here is the mechanical reason.
J5's acceptance evidence (AC1 readiness probe reports REST answering; AC2 smoke test creates and reads back an issue) can only be produced on a NATIVE amd64 runner. The available one is GitHub Actions' ubuntu-latest, via a new job in external-integration.yml. But that workflow is 'workflow_dispatch: {}' + weekly cron ONLY — it does not run on a Gerrit patchset — and GitHub is a READ-ONLY MIRROR (AGENTS.md; origin rejects direct pushes), so I cannot push a side branch there to dispatch against.
CONSEQUENCE: the new job cannot be dispatched until it EXISTS ON main. So this ticket genuinely cannot be verified before its change lands — exactly the case the skill's early-land exception describes ('behavior that only manifests post-merge').
SEQUENCE I WILL FOLLOW:
 1. earn both Gerrit gates on J5's change as normal (no shortcut there);
 2. run the explicit REGRESSION-SAFETY CHECK the exception requires, and record it here — the landing prefix is changes 1010 (J2), 1011 (J4), 1013 (J3) and J5's own, all already green on LLM-Review AND Verified with full suites passing, and J5's change is ADDITIVE-ONLY to CI (a new job; the pre-existing LLM-keyed job untouched) plus new files under tests/external/ that the default suite excludes by construction;
 3. land that prefix;
 4. dispatch external-integration.yml on main to produce the acceptance evidence;
 5. close J5 on that evidence — NOT before.
This is a deviation from 'the whole stack lands after the epic closes', taken deliberately and under the exception's stated conditions rather than for convenience. Recording it before doing it so the trail shows the reasoning, not just the outcome.

DESIGN REFINEMENT worth adding while implementing: the harness's STRUCTURAL invariants need no live Jira — that the Dockerfile pins the base by digest, keeps -DskipAllPrompts=true, and keeps -Dproduct.start.timeout, and that the compose binds loopback-only with tty/stdin_open. Those belong in a NORMAL unit test (tests/unit/) that runs on EVERY commit, not under tests/external/ where they would be skipped by the opt-in guard. Putting them in the external tier would mean the flag whose removal causes a silent 25-minute death is only checked on a weekly cron. So: live behavior -> tests/external/live_jira_dc/; file-shape enforcement -> tests/unit/. I will add the unit file to this ticket's file_impact.
